{"id":"neet-2026-q054","pn":54,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Optical isomerism in complexes","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement-I: [Fe(ox)₃]³⁻ is chiral.\nStatement-II: trans–[Cr(H₂O)₂(ox)₂]⁻ is chiral.\n(Given : oxH₂ = HOOC – COOH)\nIn light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are incorrect","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct"],"c":1,"s":"[Fe(ox)₃]³⁻ has two non-superimposable mirror images, so it is optically active and chiral. Statement-I is correct.\ntrans-[Cr(H₂O)₂(ox)₂]⁻ has a plane of symmetry.\nSo it is not chiral and is optically inactive. Statement-II is incorrect."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q071","pn":71,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Magnetic moment of complexes","q":"Among the species given below, the spin-only magnetic moment is highest for\n(Given: Atomic number of Ti = 22, Mn = 25, Fe = 26 and Co = 27)","o":["[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻","[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Mn(CN)₆]³⁻"],"c":3,"s":"Spin-only magnetic moment is highest when the number of unpaired electrons is highest.\n[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺: Ti³⁺ = [Ar] 4s⁰ 3d¹, so unpaired electrons = 1.\n[Mn(CN)₆]³⁻: Mn³⁺ = [Ar] 4s⁰ 3d⁴, CN⁻ is a strong ligand so the 4 electrons go into t₂g (2 paired, 2 unpaired), so unpaired electrons = 2.\n[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻: Fe³⁺ = [Ar] 4s⁰ 3d⁵, low spin t₂g⁵, so unpaired electrons = 1.\nHighest is [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ with 2 unpaired electrons.\nNote: Publisher's solution does not show Co³⁺ (0 unpaired electrons, low spin d⁶); answer unaffected."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q072","pn":72,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Isomerism in coordination compounds","q":"The complex which has facial and meridional isomers is\n(Given : py = pyridine and en = H₂N – CH₂ – CH₂ – NH₂)","o":["[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]³⁺","[Ni(en)₂(H₂O)₂]²⁺","[Cr(py)₃(Cl)₃]"],"c":3,"s":"Complexes of the type [Ma₃b₃] show facial and meridional isomers.\nSo [Cr(py)₃(Cl)₃] shows fac and mer isomers."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q073","pn":73,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Ionisation of coordination compounds","q":"A 1 : 3 electrolyte in an aqueous solution is","o":["[CoCl(NH₃)₅]Cl₂","[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃","[Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃]","[CoCl₂(NH₃)₄]Cl"],"c":1,"s":"[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ in water dissociates into [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ and 3Cl⁻ ions.\nSo it is a 1 : 3 electrolyte."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q078","pn":78,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Nomenclature of coordination compounds","q":"The formula of tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(III) chloride is","o":["[Co(NH₃)₄]Cl₃ × H₂O","[Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)Cl]Cl","[Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)Cl]Cl₂","[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂] × H₂O"],"c":2,"s":"Ligands: 4 ammine (NH₃), 1 aqua (H₂O), 1 chlorido (Cl⁻); central metal Co³⁺.\nCharge on the complex ion = +3 − 1 = +2, so two Cl⁻ outside the bracket.\nFormula: [Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)Cl]Cl₂."}
